<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John I Tzimiskes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 925–976</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as John Tzimiskes, Tzimiskes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John I Tzimiskes was Byzantine emperor from 969 to 976, an Armenian-born general who seized the throne by murdering his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros II Phokas</a>. He defeated the Rus prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 971, annexed eastern Bulgaria and campaigned into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. Around 972 he issued the Tragos, the first charter of the monastic community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes.jpg" alt="John I Tzimiskes" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown 10th-century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamberg_Gunthertuch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
